1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,182
In this video, we'll learn how turning
arrows and cylindrical coordinates work,

2
00:00:05,182 --> 00:00:08,828
and see how they're used to look
inside a tropical cyclone,

3
00:00:08,828 --> 00:00:12,350
and to measure how its winds
push the sea onto the coast.

4
00:00:13,150 --> 00:00:18,170
Picture an air parcel, a small volume
of air that moves with the flow,

5
00:00:18,170 --> 00:00:22,473
going round the eye of a tropical
cyclone at a steady speed,

6
00:00:22,473 --> 00:00:24,625
never faster and never slower.

7
00:00:24,975 --> 00:00:26,875
Is it accelerating?

8
00:00:27,225 --> 00:00:31,825
Hold on to your guess, because we'll
answer it with arrows that turn.

9
00:00:32,175 --> 00:00:37,925
A tropical cyclone is a huge spinning
storm that forms over a warm ocean.

10
00:00:38,275 --> 00:00:42,039
Near America it's called a hurricane,
and near India and

11
00:00:42,039 --> 00:00:46,475
Bangladesh it's called a cyclone,
but it's the same kind of storm.

12
00:00:46,825 --> 00:00:50,475
This is the second of seven
parts on coordinates.

13
00:00:50,825 --> 00:00:54,998
Part 1 gave us polar coordinates,
with their two arrows,

14
00:00:54,998 --> 00:00:58,500
r hat and theta hat, which
turn as we go round.

15
00:00:58,850 --> 00:01:01,998
In this part, we find
out how fast they turn,

16
00:01:01,998 --> 00:01:05,705
add a height to get cylindrical
coordinates, and then

17
00:01:05,705 --> 00:01:08,923
use Part 1's turned axes
to see, on real maps,

18
00:01:08,923 --> 00:01:12,350
how a storm's wind pushes
the sea onto the coast.

19
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:16,621
The next few minutes build
these rules step by step,

20
00:01:16,621 --> 00:01:19,225
for anyone who wants to see every move.

21
00:01:19,575 --> 00:01:24,304
You don't need them for the weather,
the climate science or the maps later on,

22
00:01:24,304 --> 00:01:28,003
so if it feels like too much, jump
to the time on the screen,

23
00:01:28,003 --> 00:01:30,125
and you'll get the main idea there.

24
00:01:30,475 --> 00:01:33,075
Let's start with r hat on its own.

25
00:01:33,425 --> 00:01:35,750
Why do we care how it changes?

26
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:39,460
Because we write winds
with r hat and theta hat,

27
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:43,800
and when an air parcel goes
round, these arrows turn under it.

28
00:01:44,150 --> 00:01:49,050
To find the parcel's acceleration,
we'll need to know how fast they turn.

29
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:51,850
So draw r hat from the origin.

30
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:56,648
Its length is one, so its tip
sits on a circle of radius one,

31
00:01:56,648 --> 00:01:58,325
called the unit circle.

32
00:01:58,675 --> 00:02:03,075
And the tip is exactly
the point cosine theta, sine theta.

33
00:02:03,425 --> 00:02:06,075
Now turn theta by a small amount.

34
00:02:06,425 --> 00:02:11,725
We call this small turn delta theta, where
delta just means a small change.

35
00:02:12,075 --> 00:02:14,850
The tip slides along the unit circle.

36
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:16,700
How far?

37
00:02:17,050 --> 00:02:21,513
By the arc rule, the distance
is the radius times the angle,

38
00:02:21,513 --> 00:02:23,150
and the radius is one.

39
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:26,975
So the tip slides a distance
of just delta theta.

40
00:02:28,475 --> 00:02:33,433
For example, a turn of zero point one
radians, about six degrees,

41
00:02:33,433 --> 00:02:37,475
slides the tip about
zero point one along the circle.

42
00:02:38,975 --> 00:02:41,225
And which way does it slide?

43
00:02:41,575 --> 00:02:45,742
Sliding along a circle
is at right angles to the radius,

44
00:02:45,742 --> 00:02:47,975
and theta grows anticlockwise.

45
00:02:48,325 --> 00:02:53,525
That's exactly the direction
of theta hat, as we found in Part 1.

46
00:02:53,875 --> 00:02:58,940
So, for a small turn, the tip
of r hat moves a distance delta theta,

47
00:02:58,940 --> 00:03:01,175
in the direction of theta hat.

48
00:03:01,975 --> 00:03:04,851
This little picture
gives us something that

49
00:03:04,851 --> 00:03:07,660
no earlier movie in this series has shown:

50
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:09,800
how fast cosine and sine change.

51
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:13,041
In the derivatives
movie, we met the slope,

52
00:03:13,041 --> 00:03:16,134
or derivative: the small
change in a quantity,

53
00:03:16,134 --> 00:03:20,975
divided by the small change that
caused it, as both shrink towards zero.

54
00:03:21,325 --> 00:03:26,275
The tip's east part is cosine
theta, and its north part is sine theta.

55
00:03:26,625 --> 00:03:33,175
The tip moved delta theta along theta hat,
which is minus sine theta, cosine theta.

56
00:03:33,525 --> 00:03:38,775
So its east part changes by about
minus sine theta times delta theta.

57
00:03:39,125 --> 00:03:44,150
And its north part changes by about
cosine theta times delta theta.

58
00:03:45,650 --> 00:03:51,600
Now divide both changes by delta theta,
and let delta theta shrink towards zero.

59
00:03:51,950 --> 00:03:55,675
The slope of cosine theta
is minus sine theta.

60
00:03:56,025 --> 00:03:59,425
And the slope of sine
theta is cosine theta.

61
00:04:00,925 --> 00:04:05,079
The minus sign makes sense:
turning anticlockwise from east,

62
00:04:05,079 --> 00:04:09,025
the tip moves towards the west, so
its east part shrinks.

63
00:04:09,375 --> 00:04:11,081
Let's check with numbers,

64
00:04:11,081 --> 00:04:15,311
at the balloon's angle
from Part 1, about fifty three degrees,

65
00:04:15,311 --> 00:04:19,950
where the cosine is zero point six
and the sine is zero point eight.

66
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:23,078
Turn by zero point zero one radians,

67
00:04:23,078 --> 00:04:27,400
about half a degree, to fifty
three point seven degrees.

68
00:04:27,750 --> 00:04:33,375
The cosine drops from zero point six
zero zero to zero point five nine two.

69
00:04:33,725 --> 00:04:39,225
Its change, divided by the turn, is
minus zero point eight: minus the sine.

70
00:04:40,725 --> 00:04:46,125
The sine grows from zero point eight
zero zero to zero point eight zero six.

71
00:04:46,475 --> 00:04:51,275
Its change, divided by the turn, is
zero point six: the cosine.

72
00:04:52,775 --> 00:04:54,600
So the rule works.

73
00:04:54,950 --> 00:04:59,050
These two slopes will come back
again and again in this series.

74
00:04:59,850 --> 00:05:03,825
Now let's put the two parts
back together into one arrow.

75
00:05:04,175 --> 00:05:09,525
The change in r hat is the short
arrow from its old tip to its new tip.

76
00:05:09,875 --> 00:05:12,125
We call it delta r hat.

77
00:05:12,475 --> 00:05:15,450
It's about theta hat times delta theta.

78
00:05:16,950 --> 00:05:20,500
Now divide by delta
theta, and let it shrink.

79
00:05:20,850 --> 00:05:23,925
We get the rate of change
of r hat with theta.

80
00:05:24,275 --> 00:05:28,675
We write it d r hat by d theta, and it
equals theta hat.

81
00:05:30,175 --> 00:05:35,250
The letter d is how the derivatives
movie writes a change when it's tiny.

82
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:38,935
Some books multiply both sides by d theta,

83
00:05:38,935 --> 00:05:42,350
and write d r hat equals
theta hat d theta.

84
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:44,700
It's the same statement.

85
00:05:45,050 --> 00:05:48,025
We can also check it one part at a time.

86
00:05:48,375 --> 00:05:54,575
The slopes of cosine theta and sine theta
are minus sine theta and cosine theta.

87
00:05:54,925 --> 00:06:00,075
Together, that's minus sine
theta, cosine theta, which is theta hat.

88
00:06:01,575 --> 00:06:03,625
So both routes agree.

89
00:06:03,975 --> 00:06:08,750
In words: when theta grows, r hat
swings towards theta hat.

90
00:06:09,550 --> 00:06:11,325
Now theta hat.

91
00:06:11,675 --> 00:06:14,775
How does theta hat
change when theta grows?

92
00:06:15,125 --> 00:06:18,425
Let's take this
slowly, one step at a time.

93
00:06:18,775 --> 00:06:22,625
First, theta hat is also
an arrow of length one.

94
00:06:22,975 --> 00:06:28,175
So let's draw it from the origin too, from
the same starting point as r hat.

95
00:06:28,525 --> 00:06:31,450
Its tip sits on the same unit circle.

96
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:37,375
Next, remember that theta hat
is always r hat turned by ninety degrees.

97
00:06:37,725 --> 00:06:42,275
So its tip sits on the circle a quarter
turn ahead of r hat's tip.

98
00:06:42,625 --> 00:06:45,350
Now turn theta by delta theta.

99
00:06:45,700 --> 00:06:48,850
R hat turns by delta theta, and theta hat,

100
00:06:48,850 --> 00:06:54,175
which always stays a quarter turn
ahead, turns by the same delta theta.

101
00:06:54,525 --> 00:06:59,975
So its tip also slides a distance
delta theta along the unit circle.

102
00:07:00,325 --> 00:07:02,325
Which way does it slide?

103
00:07:02,675 --> 00:07:04,410
The same rule as before:

104
00:07:04,410 --> 00:07:09,543
a tip sliding anticlockwise along
the circle moves along its own arrow,

105
00:07:09,543 --> 00:07:11,350
turned by ninety degrees.

106
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:16,750
So theta hat's tip moves along
theta hat, turned by ninety degrees.

107
00:07:18,250 --> 00:07:22,577
To find that direction, let's
turn theta hat with Part 1's rule:

108
00:07:22,577 --> 00:07:26,025
swap the two numbers,
and put a minus on the first.

109
00:07:26,375 --> 00:07:30,200
Theta hat is minus sine
theta, cosine theta.

110
00:07:30,550 --> 00:07:34,350
Swapping gives cosine
theta, minus sine theta.

111
00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:39,825
And a minus on the first gives
minus cosine theta, minus sine theta.

112
00:07:41,325 --> 00:07:47,125
That's exactly minus r hat: the arrow
r hat, flipped to point the other way.

113
00:07:48,625 --> 00:07:54,950
So, for a small turn, theta hat changes
by about minus r hat times delta theta.

114
00:07:55,300 --> 00:08:01,200
And its rate of change with theta, d theta
hat by d theta, is minus r hat.

115
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,200
So what does minus r hat
mean in the picture?

116
00:08:05,550 --> 00:08:07,496
Let's go back to the balloon,

117
00:08:07,496 --> 00:08:11,725
five kilometres from the station,
and move it round its circle.

118
00:08:12,075 --> 00:08:15,475
At each place, draw
theta hat at the balloon.

119
00:08:15,825 --> 00:08:19,600
Due east of the station,
theta hat points north.

120
00:08:19,950 --> 00:08:24,975
A little further round, it leans
to the west, towards the station's side.

121
00:08:25,325 --> 00:08:28,925
Now slide those arrows so
that their tails touch.

122
00:08:29,275 --> 00:08:30,743
Their tips swing round,

123
00:08:30,743 --> 00:08:35,850
and the small change from one arrow to
the next points back towards the station.

124
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:41,450
That's minus r hat: from the balloon,
back towards the centre of its circle.

125
00:08:41,800 --> 00:08:47,000
That's what we mean when we say that
theta hat's tip moves towards the centre.

126
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:50,575
Let's check with numbers.

127
00:08:50,925 --> 00:08:54,587
The rate of change of theta hat
is its small change,

128
00:08:54,587 --> 00:08:57,475
divided by the small turn that caused it.

129
00:08:57,825 --> 00:09:03,531
For the small turn, let's take delta theta
equal to zero point zero one radians,

130
00:09:03,531 --> 00:09:05,600
which is about half a degree.

131
00:09:05,950 --> 00:09:11,725
At fifty three degrees, theta hat
is minus zero point eight, zero point six.

132
00:09:12,075 --> 00:09:15,519
After a turn
of zero point zero one radians,

133
00:09:15,519 --> 00:09:20,450
it's minus zero point eight zero
six, zero point five nine two.

134
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:26,575
The change is minus zero point zero zero
six, minus zero point zero zero eight.

135
00:09:26,925 --> 00:09:29,735
Now divide each number by delta theta,

136
00:09:29,735 --> 00:09:33,875
and we get minus zero point six, minus
zero point eight.

137
00:09:35,375 --> 00:09:40,575
Now put it next to r hat, which
is zero point six, zero point eight.

138
00:09:40,925 --> 00:09:43,381
Every number has the same size,

139
00:09:43,381 --> 00:09:47,500
but the opposite sign, so
the answer is minus r hat.

140
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,950
We can also check it one part
at a time with the new slopes.

141
00:09:53,300 --> 00:09:57,200
The slope of minus sine
theta is minus cosine theta,

142
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:00,950
and the slope of cosine
theta is minus sine theta.

143
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:07,175
Together, that's minus cosine theta, minus
sine theta: minus r hat again.

144
00:10:08,675 --> 00:10:12,026
So here's the whole rule
in words: turn the angle,

145
00:10:12,026 --> 00:10:14,975
and each arrow swings
towards the other one.

146
00:10:15,325 --> 00:10:20,925
R hat swings towards theta hat, and
theta hat swings towards minus r hat.

147
00:10:21,725 --> 00:10:24,254
If you skipped, here's what to keep:

148
00:10:24,254 --> 00:10:27,838
as the angle grows, r hat
swings towards theta hat,

149
00:10:27,838 --> 00:10:32,475
and theta hat swings towards
minus r hat, back towards the centre.

150
00:10:32,825 --> 00:10:35,814
Next comes one more
stretch with every step,

151
00:10:35,814 --> 00:10:39,075
from a new tool all the way
to the acceleration.

152
00:10:39,425 --> 00:10:42,850
If you'd rather, jump
to the time on the screen.

153
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:46,579
Before we use these rules
on a moving air parcel,

154
00:10:46,579 --> 00:10:51,475
we need one more tool, which no
earlier movie in this series has shown.

155
00:10:51,825 --> 00:10:54,075
It's called the product rule.

156
00:10:54,425 --> 00:10:59,067
Here's the question it answers:
how does a number times an arrow change,

157
00:10:59,067 --> 00:11:01,775
when both the number and the arrow change?

158
00:11:02,125 --> 00:11:04,650
Let's try easy numbers first.

159
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,701
Take a speed of ten metres per second,

160
00:11:07,701 --> 00:11:12,250
along an arrow pointing
east, one, zero, which we'll call d hat.

161
00:11:12,600 --> 00:11:15,800
Their product is the arrow ten, zero.

162
00:11:17,300 --> 00:11:21,200
One second later, the speed
has grown to eleven.

163
00:11:21,550 --> 00:11:25,287
And the arrow has
turned by zero point one radians,

164
00:11:25,287 --> 00:11:29,025
to about
zero point nine nine five, zero point one.

165
00:11:29,375 --> 00:11:35,075
The new speed times the new arrow is about
ten point nine four five, one point one.

166
00:11:35,425 --> 00:11:40,375
So the product changed by
zero point nine four five, one point one.

167
00:11:41,875 --> 00:11:44,125
Where does that change come from?

168
00:11:44,475 --> 00:11:46,400
There are three pieces.

169
00:11:46,750 --> 00:11:51,425
First, the change in the number, times
the old arrow, gives one, zero.

170
00:11:51,775 --> 00:11:55,773
Second, the old number times
the change in the arrow:

171
00:11:55,773 --> 00:11:59,771
the arrow changed by minus
zero point zero zero five,

172
00:11:59,771 --> 00:12:04,675
zero point one, so this piece
is minus zero point zero five, one.

173
00:12:05,025 --> 00:12:11,530
Third, the two changes multiplied together
give minus zero point zero zero five,

174
00:12:11,530 --> 00:12:12,750
zero point one.

175
00:12:14,250 --> 00:12:18,399
Add the three pieces, and we
get zero point nine four five,

176
00:12:18,399 --> 00:12:20,650
one point one: the whole change.

177
00:12:22,150 --> 00:12:23,925
Now the key step.

178
00:12:24,275 --> 00:12:28,375
The third piece is a small
change times a small change.

179
00:12:28,725 --> 00:12:32,383
If we halve the time
step, the first two pieces halve,

180
00:12:32,383 --> 00:12:35,025
but the third one shrinks to a quarter.

181
00:12:35,375 --> 00:12:40,226
Divide each piece by the time step:
the first two stay at about one,

182
00:12:40,226 --> 00:12:43,152
but the third goes from zero point one to

183
00:12:43,152 --> 00:12:46,647
zero point zero five
to zero point zero two five,

184
00:12:46,647 --> 00:12:50,500
so as the time step shrinks,
the third piece vanishes.

185
00:12:50,850 --> 00:12:53,400
What's left is the product rule.

186
00:12:53,750 --> 00:12:56,256
The rate of change of a number times an

187
00:12:56,256 --> 00:12:59,019
arrow is the number's
rate times the arrow,

188
00:12:59,019 --> 00:13:01,525
plus the number times the arrow's rate.

189
00:13:03,025 --> 00:13:06,025
Now let's put an air parcel on Ian's ring,

190
00:13:06,025 --> 00:13:11,025
twenty five kilometres from the
centre, going round at a steady speed.

191
00:13:11,375 --> 00:13:14,575
How fast does the air
go round on that ring?

192
00:13:14,925 --> 00:13:17,250
We measured it in Part 1.

193
00:13:17,600 --> 00:13:21,973
At each point, we split
the wind's east and north parts,

194
00:13:21,973 --> 00:13:25,486
u and v, into a radial
and a tangential part:

195
00:13:25,486 --> 00:13:29,234
u sub r equals u cosine
theta plus v sine theta,

196
00:13:29,234 --> 00:13:34,075
and u sub theta equals minus u sine
theta plus v cosine theta.

197
00:13:34,425 --> 00:13:38,477
For example, take an east
wind of ten metres per second,

198
00:13:38,477 --> 00:13:40,358
so u is ten and v is zero,

199
00:13:40,358 --> 00:13:42,890
at the balloon's point from Part 1,

200
00:13:42,890 --> 00:13:48,100
where cosine theta is zero point six
and sine theta is zero point eight.

201
00:13:48,450 --> 00:13:52,683
Multiplying the wind's
parts by the cosine and the sine,

202
00:13:52,683 --> 00:13:58,125
as the two formulas say, u sub r is
six, and u sub theta is minus eight.

203
00:13:59,625 --> 00:14:04,827
Round Ian's ring, we did this at seventy
two points, one every five degrees,

204
00:14:04,827 --> 00:14:08,659
and the average of the seventy
two values of u sub theta

205
00:14:08,659 --> 00:14:12,150
is about thirty four point eight
metres per second.

206
00:14:12,500 --> 00:14:16,119
In this part, we'll call
that speed round the ring v,

207
00:14:16,119 --> 00:14:20,625
so from now on, v means the speed, not
the north part of the wind.

208
00:14:20,975 --> 00:14:25,025
As the parcel goes
round, its angle theta grows.

209
00:14:25,375 --> 00:14:26,950
How fast?

210
00:14:27,300 --> 00:14:30,300
Let's find out, one step at a time.

211
00:14:30,650 --> 00:14:37,350
In a short time, delta t, the parcel moves
a distance v times delta t along the ring.

212
00:14:38,850 --> 00:14:44,475
By the arc rule, a distance along a circle
is the radius times the angle.

213
00:14:44,825 --> 00:14:50,361
So the angle it turns through is that
distance divided by the radius:

214
00:14:50,361 --> 00:14:51,725
v delta t over r.

215
00:14:53,225 --> 00:14:56,675
Now divide by delta t, and let it shrink.

216
00:14:57,025 --> 00:15:01,900
The rate at which theta
grows, d theta by d t, is v over r.

217
00:15:03,400 --> 00:15:05,725
Now let's put in the numbers.

218
00:15:06,075 --> 00:15:11,504
The speed, thirty four point eight
metres per second, divided by the radius,

219
00:15:11,504 --> 00:15:13,504
twenty five thousand metres,

220
00:15:13,504 --> 00:15:18,075
is about zero point zero zero one
three nine radians per second.

221
00:15:19,575 --> 00:15:23,425
The metres cancel, and radians
per second are left.

222
00:15:23,775 --> 00:15:27,621
That's about zero point zero eight
degrees every second,

223
00:15:27,621 --> 00:15:30,025
or about five degrees every minute.

224
00:15:31,525 --> 00:15:36,875
So one full lap, two pi radians,
takes two pi divided by this rate.

225
00:15:37,225 --> 00:15:42,350
That's about four thousand five hundred
seconds, or about seventy five minutes.

226
00:15:43,850 --> 00:15:46,575
Next, the parcel's velocity.

227
00:15:46,925 --> 00:15:48,575
Why do we need it?

228
00:15:48,925 --> 00:15:52,872
Because acceleration is the rate
of change of velocity,

229
00:15:52,872 --> 00:15:55,025
so we need the velocity first.

230
00:15:55,375 --> 00:15:59,382
And velocity is the rate
of change of position: how far,

231
00:15:59,382 --> 00:16:03,175
and in which direction,
the parcel moves each second.

232
00:16:03,525 --> 00:16:08,550
So let's write the parcel's position
as an arrow from the centre of the eye.

233
00:16:08,900 --> 00:16:13,325
Its length is the distance r, and
its direction is r hat.

234
00:16:13,675 --> 00:16:16,950
So the position arrow is r times r hat.

235
00:16:18,450 --> 00:16:21,250
That's just Part 1 read backwards.

236
00:16:21,600 --> 00:16:27,575
R hat is the arrow divided by its length,
so the arrow is its length times r hat.

237
00:16:27,925 --> 00:16:32,425
For Ian's parcel, that's twenty
five kilometres times r hat.

238
00:16:32,775 --> 00:16:36,175
Velocity is the rate
of change of this arrow.

239
00:16:36,525 --> 00:16:41,775
It's a number, r, times an
arrow, r hat, so we use the product rule.

240
00:16:42,125 --> 00:16:47,739
The rate of change of r times r hat
is d r by d t times r hat,

241
00:16:47,739 --> 00:16:50,275
plus r times d r hat by d t.

242
00:16:51,775 --> 00:16:55,100
Now let's simplify, one term at a time.

243
00:16:55,450 --> 00:16:58,689
On a ring, the distance r never changes,

244
00:16:58,689 --> 00:17:02,900
so d r by d t is zero, and
the first term drops out.

245
00:17:04,400 --> 00:17:09,050
For the second term, we need
how fast r hat changes in time.

246
00:17:09,400 --> 00:17:13,625
R hat depends on theta, and theta
depends on time.

247
00:17:13,975 --> 00:17:18,700
That's a chain, so we use the chain
rule from the derivatives movie.

248
00:17:19,050 --> 00:17:25,075
D r hat by d t equals d r hat
by d theta, times d theta by d t.

249
00:17:25,425 --> 00:17:28,875
We've found both: theta hat, and v over r.

250
00:17:29,225 --> 00:17:33,650
So d r hat by d t is
theta hat times v over r.

251
00:17:35,150 --> 00:17:37,225
Now multiply by r.

252
00:17:37,575 --> 00:17:42,200
The two r's cancel, and the velocity
is v times theta hat.

253
00:17:43,700 --> 00:17:47,875
So yes: v theta hat
is the parcel's velocity.

254
00:17:48,225 --> 00:17:53,800
Its size is the speed v, and its direction
is theta hat, along the ring.

255
00:17:54,150 --> 00:17:58,828
For Ian's parcel, that's thirty
four point eight metres per second,

256
00:17:58,828 --> 00:18:02,250
along the ring,
anticlockwise as seen from above.

257
00:18:03,050 --> 00:18:06,000
Now we can answer the opening question.

258
00:18:06,350 --> 00:18:10,250
Acceleration is the rate
of change of velocity.

259
00:18:10,600 --> 00:18:13,761
So we need the rate
of change of v theta hat,

260
00:18:13,761 --> 00:18:16,500
which is a number times an arrow again.

261
00:18:16,850 --> 00:18:21,339
By the product
rule, it's d v by d t times theta hat,

262
00:18:21,339 --> 00:18:24,050
plus v times d theta hat by d t.

263
00:18:25,550 --> 00:18:28,604
The parcel goes round at a steady speed,

264
00:18:28,604 --> 00:18:32,575
so d v by d t is zero, and
the first term drops out.

265
00:18:34,075 --> 00:18:37,675
For the second term, we
use the chain rule again.

266
00:18:38,025 --> 00:18:44,275
D theta hat by d t equals d theta
hat by d theta, times d theta by d t.

267
00:18:44,625 --> 00:18:49,695
Remember the turning arrows:
d theta hat by d theta is minus r hat,

268
00:18:49,695 --> 00:18:53,100
because theta hat swings
towards minus r hat.

269
00:18:53,450 --> 00:18:56,525
And d theta by d t is v over r.

270
00:18:56,875 --> 00:19:01,775
So d theta hat by d t is
minus r hat, times v over r.

271
00:19:03,275 --> 00:19:05,250
Now multiply by v.

272
00:19:05,600 --> 00:19:10,425
The acceleration
is minus v squared over r, times r hat.

273
00:19:11,925 --> 00:19:15,801
The minus sign means that
it points along minus r hat:

274
00:19:15,801 --> 00:19:18,600
inward, towards the centre of the ring.

275
00:19:18,950 --> 00:19:22,006
An acceleration that
points to the centre of

276
00:19:22,006 --> 00:19:25,200
a circle
is called a centripetal acceleration.

277
00:19:25,550 --> 00:19:27,575
Let's check the units.

278
00:19:27,925 --> 00:19:31,675
V squared is in metres
squared per second squared.

279
00:19:32,025 --> 00:19:36,375
Dividing by r, in metres, leaves
metres per second squared,

280
00:19:36,375 --> 00:19:39,250
which are the units of an acceleration.

281
00:19:40,750 --> 00:19:43,355
If you skipped, here's what to keep:

282
00:19:43,355 --> 00:19:46,684
an air parcel going
round a circle at a steady

283
00:19:46,684 --> 00:19:50,303
speed v is always accelerating
towards the centre,

284
00:19:50,303 --> 00:19:51,750
by v squared over r.

285
00:19:52,100 --> 00:19:54,650
So how big is it for Ian's parcel?

286
00:19:55,000 --> 00:20:00,483
For Ian's ring, we square the speed,
thirty four point eight metres per second,

287
00:20:00,483 --> 00:20:04,300
then divide by the radius, twenty
five thousand metres.

288
00:20:04,650 --> 00:20:09,525
That gives about zero point zero
four eight metres per second squared.

289
00:20:11,025 --> 00:20:16,139
Rounding the speed to thirty five
gives about zero point zero four nine,

290
00:20:16,139 --> 00:20:17,275
almost the same.

291
00:20:18,775 --> 00:20:20,475
Is that big?

292
00:20:20,825 --> 00:20:24,945
Gravity is nine point eight one
metres per second squared,

293
00:20:24,945 --> 00:20:28,000
so this is about two
hundred times smaller.

294
00:20:29,500 --> 00:20:31,550
But it acts all the time.

295
00:20:31,900 --> 00:20:35,090
A car with this acceleration
would take about ten

296
00:20:35,090 --> 00:20:38,150
minutes to reach a hundred
kilometres per hour.

297
00:20:39,650 --> 00:20:42,336
Here, instead of making the parcel faster,

298
00:20:42,336 --> 00:20:45,725
all of it goes into turning
the parcel round the eye.

299
00:20:46,525 --> 00:20:50,325
So how can a steady speed
still mean an acceleration?

300
00:20:50,675 --> 00:20:54,250
The answer is that velocity
is more than speed.

301
00:20:54,600 --> 00:20:57,925
Velocity is speed and direction together.

302
00:20:58,275 --> 00:21:02,325
Let's see it with two velocity
arrows, one minute apart.

303
00:21:02,675 --> 00:21:06,980
In one minute, theta grows by
its rate times sixty seconds:

304
00:21:06,980 --> 00:21:11,650
about zero point zero eight four
radians, or about five degrees.

305
00:21:13,150 --> 00:21:17,194
Both arrows are thirty four point eight
metres per second long,

306
00:21:17,194 --> 00:21:20,275
but they point in slightly
different directions.

307
00:21:20,625 --> 00:21:23,425
Now slide them so that their tails touch.

308
00:21:23,775 --> 00:21:28,775
The short arrow from the first tip
to the second is the change in velocity.

309
00:21:29,125 --> 00:21:31,275
It points towards the eye.

310
00:21:32,775 --> 00:21:34,500
How long is it?

311
00:21:34,850 --> 00:21:39,345
Its two ends lie on a circle
of radius thirty four point eight,

312
00:21:39,345 --> 00:21:40,700
a small turn apart.

313
00:21:41,050 --> 00:21:43,963
So by the arc rule, radius times angle,

314
00:21:43,963 --> 00:21:47,250
it's about two point nine
metres per second.

315
00:21:48,750 --> 00:21:50,497
Divide by sixty seconds,

316
00:21:50,497 --> 00:21:55,591
and we get about zero point zero
four eight metres per second squared:

317
00:21:55,591 --> 00:21:59,375
the same acceleration,
found with no formula at all.

318
00:22:00,875 --> 00:22:03,729
So a steady speed means no acceleration

319
00:22:03,729 --> 00:22:06,875
only when the direction
stays the same too.

320
00:22:07,225 --> 00:22:10,625
Something must push the parcel
inward all the time.

321
00:22:10,975 --> 00:22:14,760
In a tropical cyclone,
that push is the pressure force,

322
00:22:14,760 --> 00:22:17,650
because the pressure is lowest in the eye.

323
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:23,226
The Earth's spin pushes the other way, a
little outward, about twenty times weaker,

324
00:22:23,226 --> 00:22:28,200
so the pressure force has to be a little
stronger than the turning alone needs.

325
00:22:28,550 --> 00:22:33,537
How winds balance these pushes has
its own movie later in this series:

326
00:22:33,537 --> 00:22:35,175
Wind on weather charts.

327
00:22:35,525 --> 00:22:40,046
And if you rode with the parcel, you'd
feel this as a push outward,

328
00:22:40,046 --> 00:22:45,175
called the centrifugal force, which
also has its own movie: Circular motion.

329
00:22:45,975 --> 00:22:48,750
So far, our storm has been flat.

330
00:22:49,100 --> 00:22:52,075
But a real tropical cyclone is tall.

331
00:22:52,425 --> 00:22:55,400
Near the sea, the air spirals in.

332
00:22:55,750 --> 00:23:00,925
Close to the eye, it rises in a ring
of towering clouds called the eyewall.

333
00:23:01,275 --> 00:23:06,750
And near the top, about twelve to fifteen
kilometres up, it flows out again.

334
00:23:07,100 --> 00:23:08,669
Here's Ian from above,

335
00:23:08,669 --> 00:23:13,375
seen by a weather satellite at six
in the evening, universal time.

336
00:23:13,725 --> 00:23:16,252
The clear hole in the middle is the eye,

337
00:23:16,252 --> 00:23:19,600
and the ring of bright cloud
round it is the eyewall.

338
00:23:19,950 --> 00:23:23,834
On the flat map, polar
coordinates use two numbers:

339
00:23:23,834 --> 00:23:26,500
the distance r and the angle theta.

340
00:23:26,850 --> 00:23:30,075
To describe a tall storm, we
add a third number:

341
00:23:30,075 --> 00:23:33,300
the height z, measured
straight up from the sea.

342
00:23:33,650 --> 00:23:38,941
Polar coordinates plus this height
are called cylindrical coordinates:

343
00:23:38,941 --> 00:23:40,075
r, theta and z.

344
00:23:40,425 --> 00:23:45,924
Nothing else changes: r and theta mean
exactly what they meant in Part 1,

345
00:23:45,924 --> 00:23:52,100
so x is still r cosine theta, y is still
r sine theta, and z is simply the height.

346
00:23:52,450 --> 00:23:53,459
That's because

347
00:23:53,459 --> 00:23:58,650
all the places at one distance r form
a cylinder round the storm's axis.

348
00:23:59,450 --> 00:24:03,525
In cylindrical coordinates,
the wind has three parts.

349
00:24:03,875 --> 00:24:08,725
The radial wind, u sub r, points
along r hat: in or out.

350
00:24:09,075 --> 00:24:14,575
The tangential wind, u sub theta, points
along theta hat: round the eye.

351
00:24:14,925 --> 00:24:19,625
And the vertical wind, w, points
along z hat: up or down.

352
00:24:19,975 --> 00:24:24,643
Written as one arrow, the wind
is u sub r times r hat,

353
00:24:24,643 --> 00:24:29,225
plus u sub theta times theta
hat, plus w times z hat.

354
00:24:29,575 --> 00:24:32,117
Z hat points straight up everywhere,

355
00:24:32,117 --> 00:24:36,425
just like x hat points east
everywhere, so z hat never turns.

356
00:24:36,775 --> 00:24:42,800
Only r hat and theta hat turn as we go
round, by the same rules we found today.

357
00:24:43,150 --> 00:24:48,050
So everything we've learned about turning
arrows works in three dimensions too.

358
00:24:48,850 --> 00:24:52,857
Before we look inside Ian, let's
check where its eye really was,

359
00:24:52,857 --> 00:24:55,925
because different instruments
see it differently.

360
00:24:56,275 --> 00:25:01,350
A weather radar on the coast near Tampa
measures the rain round the storm.

361
00:25:01,700 --> 00:25:07,050
At six in the evening, its picture
shows a ring of heavy rain, the eyewall,

362
00:25:07,050 --> 00:25:11,900
about thirty kilometres in radius, round
an eye with almost no rain.

363
00:25:12,250 --> 00:25:15,275
This is the best picture
of where the eye was.

364
00:25:16,775 --> 00:25:22,264
The weather model analysis that gives us
Ian's winds in three dimensions puts its

365
00:25:22,264 --> 00:25:27,550
own calm centre about seventeen kilometres
west-south-west of the radar's eye.

366
00:25:27,900 --> 00:25:32,053
And its lowest pressure is nine
hundred and sixty hectopascals,

367
00:25:32,053 --> 00:25:36,075
while the official estimate
is nine hundred and thirty eight.

368
00:25:37,575 --> 00:25:41,675
So the model is close, but a little
off, and a little too weak.

369
00:25:42,025 --> 00:25:46,320
That's normal: a model with grid
points every three kilometres

370
00:25:46,320 --> 00:25:49,700
can't hold the sharpest
part of a storm exactly.

371
00:25:50,050 --> 00:25:51,900
So here's our rule.

372
00:25:52,250 --> 00:25:56,200
For where the eye is, we use
the radar and the satellite.

373
00:25:56,550 --> 00:26:01,575
For the winds, we use the model,
measured round the model's own centre.

374
00:26:02,375 --> 00:26:05,900
Now let's look inside Ian, layer by layer.

375
00:26:06,250 --> 00:26:09,606
Weather models store
the wind on pressure levels:

376
00:26:09,606 --> 00:26:12,756
surfaces where the air
pressure has one value,

377
00:26:12,756 --> 00:26:15,975
like nine hundred and twenty
five hectopascals.

378
00:26:16,325 --> 00:26:18,629
Since the pressure drops as we go up,

379
00:26:18,629 --> 00:26:22,800
each level sits at a height, and we'll
write that height beside it.

380
00:26:23,150 --> 00:26:28,575
Here are five layers round Ian's eye, from
the model, at six in the evening.

381
00:26:28,925 --> 00:26:33,200
Each layer is a square about
five hundred kilometres across.

382
00:26:33,550 --> 00:26:37,827
The lowest is at nine hundred
and twenty five hectopascals,

383
00:26:37,827 --> 00:26:40,800
about seven hundred metres above the sea.

384
00:26:41,150 --> 00:26:43,613
We don't use a lower level, because near

385
00:26:43,613 --> 00:26:48,478
the eye the pressure at the sea surface
was below nine hundred and seventy five

386
00:26:48,478 --> 00:26:52,050
hectopascals, so that level
would lie under the sea there.

387
00:26:52,400 --> 00:26:55,620
Then come eight hundred
and fifty hectopascals,

388
00:26:55,620 --> 00:26:58,977
about one point four
kilometres up, five hundred,

389
00:26:58,977 --> 00:27:03,019
about six kilometres, two hundred,
about twelve kilometres,

390
00:27:03,019 --> 00:27:06,650
and one hundred and fifty, about
fourteen kilometres.

391
00:27:08,150 --> 00:27:12,425
The colour on each layer
is the radial wind, u sub r.

392
00:27:12,775 --> 00:27:17,800
Rose, with a minus sign, means that
the air flows in, towards the eye.

393
00:27:18,150 --> 00:27:21,775
Blue, with a plus sign, means
that it flows out.

394
00:27:22,125 --> 00:27:27,975
Pale means neither, and a dark line marks
where the radial wind is exactly zero.

395
00:27:28,325 --> 00:27:30,438
We drew the layers evenly spaced,

396
00:27:30,438 --> 00:27:35,175
and much farther apart than they really
are, so that we can read each one.

397
00:27:35,975 --> 00:27:39,561
Let's light the layers one
at a time, from the bottom up,

398
00:27:39,561 --> 00:27:43,650
and read each one on a ring a hundred
kilometres from the centre.

399
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:47,593
On the lowest layer, about
seven hundred metres up,

400
00:27:47,593 --> 00:27:50,200
the air flows in on most of the ring.

401
00:27:50,550 --> 00:27:54,838
Round the ring, the radial wind
averages about minus six and

402
00:27:54,838 --> 00:27:56,625
a half metres per second.

403
00:27:58,125 --> 00:28:01,700
At one point four kilometres,
it's almost zero.

404
00:28:02,050 --> 00:28:07,375
At six and at twelve kilometres,
it's a little positive, about plus one.

405
00:28:07,725 --> 00:28:11,102
And at fourteen kilometres,
the sign has flipped:

406
00:28:11,102 --> 00:28:15,650
the air flows out, at about plus six
and a half metres per second.

407
00:28:17,150 --> 00:28:21,350
So the air comes in near
the bottom, and goes out near the top.

408
00:28:21,700 --> 00:28:25,850
Now let's swing round
the stack, to see it from another side.

409
00:28:26,200 --> 00:28:29,434
And here's one short, closer
look from the side,

410
00:28:29,434 --> 00:28:32,600
just above the sea,
looking up past the layers.

411
00:28:33,400 --> 00:28:35,389
Five layers still leave gaps,

412
00:28:35,389 --> 00:28:39,025
and cylindrical coordinates
give us a neater picture.

413
00:28:39,375 --> 00:28:43,002
On every level, we can
average round each ring,

414
00:28:43,002 --> 00:28:46,475
just as we averaged
round one ring in Part 1.

415
00:28:46,825 --> 00:28:51,003
An average round a ring depends
only on the distance r and

416
00:28:51,003 --> 00:28:55,325
the height z, because going all
the way round removes theta.

417
00:28:56,825 --> 00:28:59,630
So we can draw the whole
storm on one slice,

418
00:28:59,630 --> 00:29:04,475
with the distance from the centre along
the bottom, and the height going up.

419
00:29:04,825 --> 00:29:07,925
This picture
is called a radius-height section.

420
00:29:08,275 --> 00:29:11,753
Each colour is a ring
average of the radial wind,

421
00:29:11,753 --> 00:29:14,450
now from all nine levels of the model.

422
00:29:14,800 --> 00:29:18,625
Near the sea, there's a thin
layer of strong inflow.

423
00:29:18,975 --> 00:29:23,692
Its strongest ring average is about
minus sixteen metres per second,

424
00:29:23,692 --> 00:29:28,200
thirty kilometres from the centre, about
seven hundred metres up.

425
00:29:29,700 --> 00:29:32,884
Near the top, from about
twelve kilometres up,

426
00:29:32,884 --> 00:29:37,175
the air flows out, at up to about
plus nine metres per second.

427
00:29:38,675 --> 00:29:41,725
The dark lines show the tangential wind.

428
00:29:42,075 --> 00:29:46,625
Its strongest ring average is about
fifty five metres per second,

429
00:29:46,625 --> 00:29:51,875
also thirty kilometres from the centre,
about one point four kilometres up.

430
00:29:53,375 --> 00:29:55,809
It's weaker right at the sea surface,

431
00:29:55,809 --> 00:29:59,625
where the sea slows the air, and it
gets weaker higher up.

432
00:29:59,975 --> 00:30:01,850
Here's a quick check.

433
00:30:02,200 --> 00:30:07,288
Air flows in through the side
of a cylinder thirty kilometres in radius,

434
00:30:07,288 --> 00:30:12,375
in a layer about one kilometre
deep, at about sixteen metres per second.

435
00:30:12,725 --> 00:30:16,193
All of it has to leave
through the cylinder's lid,

436
00:30:16,193 --> 00:30:19,869
which is a much bigger
area, so it rises more slowly:

437
00:30:19,869 --> 00:30:22,574
twice the depth times the inflow speed,

438
00:30:22,574 --> 00:30:26,250
divided by the radius, is about
one metre per second.

439
00:30:27,750 --> 00:30:31,354
And radar on a hurricane-hunter
aircraft measured it:

440
00:30:31,354 --> 00:30:33,395
about three metres per second,

441
00:30:33,395 --> 00:30:37,475
eight and a half kilometres
up, with single towers near ten.

442
00:30:38,975 --> 00:30:42,175
It rises in the eyewall, close to the eye.

443
00:30:42,525 --> 00:30:47,382
In, up and out: that's a tropical
cyclone's overturning circulation,

444
00:30:47,382 --> 00:30:51,025
and cylindrical coordinates
show it in one picture.

445
00:30:51,375 --> 00:30:54,252
How a tropical cyclone keeps this engine

446
00:30:54,252 --> 00:30:57,633
running has its own movie
later in this series:

447
00:30:57,633 --> 00:30:59,575
How tropical cyclones form.

448
00:31:00,375 --> 00:31:04,732
Now let's meet our second
storm, on the other side of the world,

449
00:31:04,732 --> 00:31:07,387
in the Bay of Bengal, between India and

450
00:31:07,387 --> 00:31:10,450
Myanmar, with Bangladesh
at its northern end.

451
00:31:10,800 --> 00:31:15,325
Remember, here a tropical cyclone
is simply called a cyclone.

452
00:31:15,675 --> 00:31:20,500
In May twenty twenty, Cyclone Amphan
formed in the south of the Bay.

453
00:31:20,850 --> 00:31:22,562
On the eighteenth of May,

454
00:31:22,562 --> 00:31:27,971
its winds reached the top class on the
India Meteorological Department's scale,

455
00:31:27,971 --> 00:31:30,025
called a super cyclonic storm.

456
00:31:30,375 --> 00:31:36,075
No storm in the Bay of Bengal had reached
that class since nineteen ninety nine.

457
00:31:36,425 --> 00:31:42,050
That class measures only the strongest
wind over the sea, not the harm on land.

458
00:31:42,400 --> 00:31:46,134
In between, weaker storms
hit Bangladesh very hard:

459
00:31:46,134 --> 00:31:48,917
Cyclone Sidr in two thousand and seven

460
00:31:48,917 --> 00:31:53,969
killed thousands of people, and Cyclone
Aila in two thousand and nine

461
00:31:53,969 --> 00:31:56,825
flooded Khulna and Satkhira for months.

462
00:31:57,175 --> 00:32:00,620
On the twentieth, it came
ashore in the Sundarbans,

463
00:32:00,620 --> 00:32:03,728
the huge mangrove forest
on the border between

464
00:32:03,728 --> 00:32:06,700
India's state of West
Bengal and Bangladesh.

465
00:32:07,050 --> 00:32:10,300
Its centre then passed close to Kolkata.

466
00:32:10,650 --> 00:32:14,542
It passed about forty five
kilometres west of Satkhira,

467
00:32:14,542 --> 00:32:19,425
and about seventy nine kilometres
west of Khulna, both in Bangladesh.

468
00:32:19,775 --> 00:32:25,000
So the Khulna and Satkhira districts
of Bangladesh took much of its flooding.

469
00:32:25,800 --> 00:32:28,562
Here's Amphan on the eighteenth of May,

470
00:32:28,562 --> 00:32:32,175
two days before landfall,
seen by a NASA satellite.

471
00:32:32,525 --> 00:32:37,275
In the middle of its clouds
is a tiny clear dot: a pinhole eye.

472
00:32:37,625 --> 00:32:42,283
It's the same shape as Ian, a round
storm turning round a calm eye,

473
00:32:42,283 --> 00:32:45,550
so the same cylindrical
coordinates fit it too.

474
00:32:46,350 --> 00:32:50,225
Let's measure Amphan's winds
the same way we did Ian's.

475
00:32:50,575 --> 00:32:54,848
There's no fine model like
Ian's over the Bay of Bengal,

476
00:32:54,848 --> 00:32:57,900
so we use a global data set called ERA5.

477
00:32:58,250 --> 00:33:01,862
It's a reanalysis: a best
estimate of past weather,

478
00:33:01,862 --> 00:33:05,475
made by blending observations
with a weather model.

479
00:33:05,825 --> 00:33:09,702
Its grid boxes are about
twenty eight kilometres wide,

480
00:33:09,702 --> 00:33:13,650
wider than Amphan's eye, so
it smooths the storm a lot.

481
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:17,829
Here's Amphan at six
in the morning, universal time,

482
00:33:17,829 --> 00:33:22,100
on the twentieth of May, about
four hours before landfall.

483
00:33:22,450 --> 00:33:25,565
We put the origin at ERA5's own centre,

484
00:33:25,565 --> 00:33:31,075
and draw a ring fifty kilometres
out, where ERA5's spin is strongest.

485
00:33:31,425 --> 00:33:33,223
Ten metres above the sea,

486
00:33:33,223 --> 00:33:37,537
the tangential wind averages
about twenty metres per second,

487
00:33:37,537 --> 00:33:40,125
and the radial wind about minus six.

488
00:33:41,625 --> 00:33:45,189
The air goes round anticlockwise
as seen from above,

489
00:33:45,189 --> 00:33:49,850
just like Ian's, because both storms
are in the northern hemisphere.

490
00:33:50,200 --> 00:33:52,569
The official estimate at that hour,

491
00:33:52,569 --> 00:33:56,089
averaged over three minutes,
was about ninety knots,

492
00:33:56,089 --> 00:33:59,000
which is about forty
six metres per second,

493
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:03,400
and a lowest pressure of nine
hundred and fifty six hectopascals.

494
00:34:03,750 --> 00:34:08,715
ERA5's lowest pressure is only
about nine hundred and sixty seven,

495
00:34:08,715 --> 00:34:11,800
so the reanalysis storm is much too weak.

496
00:34:13,300 --> 00:34:15,275
But the shape is right.

497
00:34:15,625 --> 00:34:19,122
The radial wind over the
tangential wind gives

498
00:34:19,122 --> 00:34:22,923
Amphan an inflow angle
of about seventeen degrees,

499
00:34:22,923 --> 00:34:24,900
close to Ian's twenty one.

500
00:34:26,400 --> 00:34:32,000
Two storms, in two different oceans, both
spiral in at a similar angle.

501
00:34:32,800 --> 00:34:37,850
Here's Amphan's radius-height
section from ERA5, next to Ian's.

502
00:34:38,200 --> 00:34:40,350
The same pattern appears.

503
00:34:40,700 --> 00:34:46,347
Near the sea, the air flows in, at up
to about minus eleven metres per second,

504
00:34:46,347 --> 00:34:49,025
a hundred kilometres from the centre.

505
00:34:50,525 --> 00:34:54,450
Near the top, it flows
out, at up to about plus six.

506
00:34:55,950 --> 00:34:59,658
The thin dark lines on both
pictures mark where the

507
00:34:59,658 --> 00:35:03,948
ring-average radial wind
is exactly zero metres per second:

508
00:35:03,948 --> 00:35:07,875
the border between air flowing
in and air flowing out.

509
00:35:08,225 --> 00:35:12,348
In the middle of the storm, the air
hardly moves in or out,

510
00:35:12,348 --> 00:35:16,750
so this border wanders, and it
wraps round small, weak pockets.

511
00:35:17,100 --> 00:35:21,571
Those pockets differ between the two
storms and the two data sets,

512
00:35:21,571 --> 00:35:25,025
so the lines don't match, but
the big pattern does.

513
00:35:25,375 --> 00:35:29,125
Where a picture has no colour
at all, there's no data.

514
00:35:29,475 --> 00:35:33,908
Close to the centre, the lowest
levels lie below the sea surface,

515
00:35:33,908 --> 00:35:37,250
and along the top, we're
above the highest level.

516
00:35:37,600 --> 00:35:42,975
Amphan's picture also starts twenty
five kilometres out, at its first ring.

517
00:35:43,325 --> 00:35:49,450
It's blurred, because ERA5's boxes are
wide, but it's the same in, up and out.

518
00:35:49,800 --> 00:35:53,184
With cylindrical coordinates,
two different storms,

519
00:35:53,184 --> 00:35:56,900
from two different data
sets, fit into the same picture.

520
00:35:57,700 --> 00:36:03,600
So why do scientists who study tropical
cyclones use cylindrical coordinates?

521
00:36:03,950 --> 00:36:06,125
There are three good reasons.

522
00:36:06,475 --> 00:36:08,300
The first is shape.

523
00:36:08,650 --> 00:36:11,190
A tropical cyclone is nearly round,

524
00:36:11,190 --> 00:36:14,239
so averaging round the rings turns a messy

525
00:36:14,239 --> 00:36:17,650
storm in three dimensions
into one clear slice.

526
00:36:18,000 --> 00:36:20,050
The second is physics.

527
00:36:20,400 --> 00:36:23,716
With turning arrows, the
inward acceleration,

528
00:36:23,716 --> 00:36:28,875
minus v squared over r, appears
the moment we write the velocity down.

529
00:36:29,225 --> 00:36:31,186
With east and north arrows,

530
00:36:31,186 --> 00:36:35,325
the same acceleration hides
inside changes of both parts.

531
00:36:35,675 --> 00:36:37,600
The third is meaning.

532
00:36:37,950 --> 00:36:42,778
The three parts, in, round and up, are
exactly what we want to know:

533
00:36:42,778 --> 00:36:48,175
how fast the storm draws air in, how fast
it spins, and where the air rises.

534
00:36:48,975 --> 00:36:54,085
Now let's come back down to the
ground, to the landfall of Hurricane Ian,

535
00:36:54,085 --> 00:36:58,075
the storm we measured
in Part 1, on the coast of Florida.

536
00:36:58,425 --> 00:37:02,900
On a coast, east and north are
not the most useful directions.

537
00:37:03,250 --> 00:37:07,385
What matters is how much
of the wind blows along the shore,

538
00:37:07,385 --> 00:37:11,450
and how much blows across
it, onto the land or out to sea.

539
00:37:11,800 --> 00:37:16,887
To split a wind that way, we turn
our axes to line up with the coast,

540
00:37:16,887 --> 00:37:19,025
just as we learned in Part 1.

541
00:37:19,375 --> 00:37:22,918
There, we turned
the x axis by an angle alpha,

542
00:37:22,918 --> 00:37:27,000
measured anticlockwise
from east, as seen from above.

543
00:37:27,350 --> 00:37:32,987
The new axis, x hat prime, is cosine
alpha, sine alpha, and y hat prime,

544
00:37:32,987 --> 00:37:38,625
a quarter turn anticlockwise from
it, is minus sine alpha, cosine alpha.

545
00:37:38,975 --> 00:37:43,054
And we derived the coordinates
in the turned axes:

546
00:37:43,054 --> 00:37:46,969
x prime equals x cosine
alpha plus y sine alpha,

547
00:37:46,969 --> 00:37:51,700
and y prime equals minus x sine
alpha plus y cosine alpha.

548
00:37:52,050 --> 00:37:57,550
In matrix form, that's one rotation
matrix times the column x, y.

549
00:37:57,900 --> 00:38:01,939
A wind is an arrow too, so
the same rule works for it,

550
00:38:01,939 --> 00:38:06,875
with x and y replaced by the wind's
east and north parts, u and v.

551
00:38:07,225 --> 00:38:12,562
So the wind's part along the shore
is u cosine alpha plus v sine alpha,

552
00:38:12,562 --> 00:38:17,975
and its part across the shore
is minus u sine alpha plus v cosine alpha.

553
00:38:18,325 --> 00:38:23,750
We'll point x hat prime along the coast,
so that y hat prime points out to sea.

554
00:38:24,100 --> 00:38:27,656
Then a positive across-shore
part blows out to sea,

555
00:38:27,656 --> 00:38:30,375
and a negative one blows onto the land.

556
00:38:30,725 --> 00:38:34,975
So everything hangs on one number:
the coast's angle, alpha.

557
00:38:35,775 --> 00:38:39,200
Which way does the coast
run near Fort Myers Beach?

558
00:38:39,550 --> 00:38:42,953
Here's the coast on a flat
map marked in kilometres,

559
00:38:42,953 --> 00:38:47,600
with two points on it: Naples
in the south, and Englewood in the north.

560
00:38:47,950 --> 00:38:51,052
Going up the coast
from Naples to Englewood,

561
00:38:51,052 --> 00:38:54,789
we move about ninety
one point five kilometres north,

562
00:38:54,789 --> 00:38:56,975
and fifty five kilometres west.

563
00:38:57,325 --> 00:39:03,156
So the coast leans west of north, by an
angle whose tangent is the opposite side,

564
00:39:03,156 --> 00:39:06,900
the west leg, over the adjacent
side, the north leg.

565
00:39:07,250 --> 00:39:13,000
That's about zero point six zero one, and
the angle is about thirty one degrees.

566
00:39:14,500 --> 00:39:19,764
So the coast runs thirty one degrees west
of north: measured clockwise from north,

567
00:39:19,764 --> 00:39:24,000
that's a compass bearing of three
hundred and twenty nine degrees.

568
00:39:25,500 --> 00:39:31,274
In Part 1, we found that a compass bearing
is ninety degrees minus the maths angle,

569
00:39:31,274 --> 00:39:35,100
so the maths angle is ninety
degrees minus the bearing.

570
00:39:35,450 --> 00:39:40,148
So alpha is ninety degrees minus the
bearing, which comes out negative,

571
00:39:40,148 --> 00:39:44,250
and adding a full turn gives a hundred
and twenty one degrees.

572
00:39:45,750 --> 00:39:48,495
The cosine of a hundred and twenty one

573
00:39:48,495 --> 00:39:51,963
degrees is about
minus zero point five one five,

574
00:39:51,963 --> 00:39:55,575
and its sine is about
zero point eight five seven.

575
00:39:55,925 --> 00:39:59,859
So x hat prime is about
minus zero point five one five,

576
00:39:59,859 --> 00:40:05,725
zero point eight five seven: it points up
the coast, towards the north-north-west.

577
00:40:06,075 --> 00:40:10,500
And y hat prime is about
minus zero point eight five seven,

578
00:40:10,500 --> 00:40:12,825
minus zero point five one five:

579
00:40:12,825 --> 00:40:16,950
it points west-south-west,
out into the Gulf of Mexico.

580
00:40:18,450 --> 00:40:22,414
That's why we picked
the north-north-west way along the coast,

581
00:40:22,414 --> 00:40:26,825
and not the south-south-east way:
then y hat prime points out to sea.

582
00:40:27,625 --> 00:40:29,250
Now the wind.

583
00:40:29,600 --> 00:40:34,340
At eight in the evening, universal
time, about an hour after landfall,

584
00:40:34,340 --> 00:40:39,621
the model's wind at Fort Myers Beach was
about thirty three metres per second,

585
00:40:39,621 --> 00:40:40,975
from the south-west.

586
00:40:41,325 --> 00:40:46,026
Its east part, u, was about twenty
two point two metres per second,

587
00:40:46,026 --> 00:40:50,025
and its north part, v, was
about twenty four point three.

588
00:40:50,375 --> 00:40:54,972
The part along the shore
is u cosine alpha plus v sine alpha:

589
00:40:54,972 --> 00:40:58,966
we multiply the east
part, u, by the cosine of alpha,

590
00:40:58,966 --> 00:41:02,809
minus zero point five one five, and
the north part,

591
00:41:02,809 --> 00:41:06,125
v, by its
sine, zero point eight five seven.

592
00:41:06,475 --> 00:41:11,200
Adding the two products gives about
nine point four metres per second.

593
00:41:12,700 --> 00:41:17,911
The part across the shore is minus u sine
alpha plus v cosine alpha:

594
00:41:17,911 --> 00:41:22,050
now u goes with minus the
sine, and v with the cosine.

595
00:41:22,400 --> 00:41:25,041
Adding these two products gives about

596
00:41:25,041 --> 00:41:28,325
minus thirty one point five
metres per second.

597
00:41:29,825 --> 00:41:35,525
The minus sign means against y hat prime:
not out to sea, but onto the land.

598
00:41:35,875 --> 00:41:40,967
So about thirty one point five metres
per second blew straight onto the land,

599
00:41:40,967 --> 00:41:44,075
and only about nine point four
along the coast.

600
00:41:44,425 --> 00:41:48,258
Let's check that it's the same
wind: we square the along and

601
00:41:48,258 --> 00:41:51,387
across parts, add
them, and take the square root,

602
00:41:51,387 --> 00:41:53,942
which gives about thirty two point nine,

603
00:41:53,942 --> 00:41:57,200
the thirty three metres
per second we started with.

604
00:41:58,700 --> 00:42:02,184
Now go about a hundred
and sixty kilometres north,

605
00:42:02,184 --> 00:42:07,200
to Saint Petersburg, on Tampa
Bay, on the other side of the storm's eye.

606
00:42:07,550 --> 00:42:10,973
Next to it, the Gulf coast
runs almost due north:

607
00:42:10,973 --> 00:42:13,768
from Saint Pete Beach to Tarpon Springs,

608
00:42:13,768 --> 00:42:17,610
it moves about fifty
five point seven kilometres north,

609
00:42:17,610 --> 00:42:20,125
and six point eight kilometres west.

610
00:42:20,475 --> 00:42:24,082
With the same tangent
rule, west leg over north leg,

611
00:42:24,082 --> 00:42:27,896
the coast leans only about
seven degrees west of north,

612
00:42:27,896 --> 00:42:31,850
a bearing of about three
hundred and fifty three degrees.

613
00:42:32,200 --> 00:42:35,283
So alpha, ninety degrees
minus the bearing,

614
00:42:35,283 --> 00:42:38,724
plus a full turn, is about
ninety seven degrees,

615
00:42:38,724 --> 00:42:42,667
with a cosine of about
minus zero point one two two and

616
00:42:42,667 --> 00:42:45,750
a sine of about
zero point nine nine three.

617
00:42:47,250 --> 00:42:50,093
The model's wind there, at the same hour,

618
00:42:50,093 --> 00:42:54,461
was about nineteen metres per second
from the north-north-east:

619
00:42:54,461 --> 00:42:58,275
u about minus ten point two, and v about
minus sixteen.

620
00:42:58,625 --> 00:43:04,825
Across the shore, we take minus u sine
alpha, and v cosine alpha, and add them.

621
00:43:05,175 --> 00:43:09,550
Together, that's about
plus twelve point one metres per second.

622
00:43:11,050 --> 00:43:14,700
Plus means along y hat prime: out to sea.

623
00:43:15,050 --> 00:43:16,225
Along the shore,

624
00:43:16,225 --> 00:43:21,735
u cosine alpha plus v sine alpha comes
to about minus fourteen point seven,

625
00:43:21,735 --> 00:43:25,775
so the wind also ran down
the coast, towards the south.

626
00:43:27,275 --> 00:43:32,656
So here's the same storm, on opposite
sides of its eye: at Fort Myers Beach,

627
00:43:32,656 --> 00:43:37,825
the wind blew onto the land, and at Saint
Petersburg, it blew out to sea.

628
00:43:38,625 --> 00:43:40,700
What does that do to the sea?

629
00:43:41,050 --> 00:43:44,918
Wind blowing onto the land
drags the sea surface with it,

630
00:43:44,918 --> 00:43:47,700
and the water piles up against the coast.

631
00:43:48,050 --> 00:43:53,650
Wind blowing out to sea drags the water
away from the coast, and the level drops.

632
00:43:54,000 --> 00:43:58,649
Where the water piles up, the sea
rises far above the normal tide,

633
00:43:58,649 --> 00:44:01,325
and this rise is called a storm surge.

634
00:44:01,675 --> 00:44:05,907
The wind's push does most of the work,
especially over a wide,

635
00:44:05,907 --> 00:44:09,525
shallow sea floor like
the one off southwest Florida.

636
00:44:09,875 --> 00:44:11,978
The low pressure helps a little:

637
00:44:11,978 --> 00:44:17,500
the sea rises by about one centimetre for
every hectopascal that the pressure drops.

638
00:44:17,850 --> 00:44:21,402
When Ian came ashore, its centre's
pressure was about

639
00:44:21,402 --> 00:44:24,082
nine hundred and forty one hectopascals,

640
00:44:24,082 --> 00:44:27,768
seventy two below the normal
one thousand and thirteen,

641
00:44:27,768 --> 00:44:32,325
so the sea under the eye rose by only
about seventy two centimetres.

642
00:44:32,675 --> 00:44:36,100
So what should the water have
done at our two stations?

643
00:44:36,900 --> 00:44:42,300
Tide gauges, which measure the water
level every few minutes, give the answer.

644
00:44:42,650 --> 00:44:44,674
At Fort Myers, up the river,

645
00:44:44,674 --> 00:44:49,300
the water rose about two point two
metres above the normal tide.

646
00:44:49,650 --> 00:44:55,098
At Saint Petersburg, it fell about one
point eight metres below the normal tide,

647
00:44:55,098 --> 00:44:57,550
both within a few hours of landfall.

648
00:44:57,900 --> 00:45:02,200
The across-shore wind flipped
its sign, and so did the water.

649
00:45:02,550 --> 00:45:03,944
At Fort Myers Beach,

650
00:45:03,944 --> 00:45:08,752
the official report found water ten
to fifteen feet above the ground:

651
00:45:08,752 --> 00:45:11,400
about three to four and a half metres.

652
00:45:12,900 --> 00:45:15,700
What did satellites see of Ian's surge?

653
00:45:16,050 --> 00:45:17,850
Almost nothing.

654
00:45:18,200 --> 00:45:21,981
The surge peaked in the evening,
under the storm's clouds,

655
00:45:21,981 --> 00:45:24,654
and drained back to the sea within hours,

656
00:45:24,654 --> 00:45:27,001
so the pictures taken from space and

657
00:45:27,001 --> 00:45:31,825
from survey planes in the next days
show the damage, not the surge itself.

658
00:45:32,175 --> 00:45:35,425
So the two gauge lines
are the surge, measured.

659
00:45:35,775 --> 00:45:38,189
The wind along a coast matters too,

660
00:45:38,189 --> 00:45:41,362
because it can bring
cold water up from below,

661
00:45:41,362 --> 00:45:45,500
which is a story for the movie
on Friction and Ekman layers.

662
00:45:45,850 --> 00:45:50,675
And the full story of storm surge has
its own movie later in this series.

663
00:45:51,475 --> 00:45:55,025
Floods that last longer
can be seen from space.

664
00:45:55,375 --> 00:46:01,075
Let's fly to one: the Bay of Bengal, after
Cyclone Amphan in twenty twenty.

665
00:46:01,425 --> 00:46:04,550
The blue gets darker
where the sea gets deeper.

666
00:46:04,900 --> 00:46:08,566
The white line marks where
the sea is twenty metres deep,

667
00:46:08,566 --> 00:46:11,075
and the darker line two hundred metres.

668
00:46:11,425 --> 00:46:12,682
Off the Sundarbans,

669
00:46:12,682 --> 00:46:15,724
the sea stays shallower
than twenty metres for

670
00:46:15,724 --> 00:46:18,700
about thirty three
kilometres from the coast.

671
00:46:20,200 --> 00:46:25,165
A wide, shallow shelf like this
lets a storm pile the water up high,

672
00:46:25,165 --> 00:46:26,625
just as off Florida.

673
00:46:26,975 --> 00:46:30,496
Here's Amphan's track, coming
up from the south of the Bay,

674
00:46:30,496 --> 00:46:34,375
and crossing the coast in the Sundarbans
on the twentieth of May.

675
00:46:35,175 --> 00:46:40,475
Before we look for Amphan's flood, let's
see which way its wind blew on the coast.

676
00:46:40,825 --> 00:46:45,123
We use ERA5 again, at nine
in the morning, universal time,

677
00:46:45,123 --> 00:46:49,050
on the twentieth of May, as Amphan
reached the coast.

678
00:46:49,400 --> 00:46:52,024
Remember that ERA5 smooths the storm:

679
00:46:52,024 --> 00:46:57,769
its strongest wind near Amphan is only
about six tenths of the official estimate,

680
00:46:57,769 --> 00:47:02,450
so we use it for the direction
of the wind, not its full strength.

681
00:47:02,800 --> 00:47:07,775
We pick a point on the Sundarbans shore
of Bangladesh, east of the centre.

682
00:47:08,125 --> 00:47:10,360
Going west along the coast there,

683
00:47:10,360 --> 00:47:14,084
we move about ninety
two point nine kilometres west and

684
00:47:14,084 --> 00:47:16,725
twenty one point nine kilometres south.

685
00:47:17,075 --> 00:47:20,539
The south leg over the west
leg gives the tangent,

686
00:47:20,539 --> 00:47:24,350
so the coast runs about
thirteen degrees south of west.

687
00:47:24,700 --> 00:47:26,671
Measured clockwise from north,

688
00:47:26,671 --> 00:47:30,350
that's a bearing of two hundred
and fifty seven degrees.

689
00:47:30,700 --> 00:47:33,700
So alpha, ninety degrees
minus the bearing,

690
00:47:33,700 --> 00:47:38,025
plus a full turn, is about a hundred
and ninety three degrees,

691
00:47:38,025 --> 00:47:42,141
with a cosine of about
minus zero point nine seven four and

692
00:47:42,141 --> 00:47:45,350
a sine of about
minus zero point two two five.

693
00:47:45,700 --> 00:47:49,100
Then y hat prime points south, out to sea.

694
00:47:49,450 --> 00:47:54,355
ERA5's wind there was about twenty
two point six metres per second,

695
00:47:54,355 --> 00:47:56,258
from the south-south-east:

696
00:47:56,258 --> 00:48:00,650
u about minus eight point four, and v
about plus twenty one.

697
00:48:01,000 --> 00:48:07,200
Across the shore, we take minus u sine
alpha, and v cosine alpha, and add them.

698
00:48:07,550 --> 00:48:11,089
Together, that's about
minus twenty two point four

699
00:48:11,089 --> 00:48:13,425
metres per second: onto the land.

700
00:48:14,925 --> 00:48:19,953
The official lowest pressure was about
nine hundred and sixty hectopascals,

701
00:48:19,953 --> 00:48:23,975
so the sea under the eye rose
about fifty three centimetres.

702
00:48:24,325 --> 00:48:28,461
So Amphan's wind pushed the sea
onto the Sundarbans coast,

703
00:48:28,461 --> 00:48:30,600
over that wide, shallow shelf.

704
00:48:30,950 --> 00:48:34,785
There's no tide-gauge record
of the water there in our data,

705
00:48:34,785 --> 00:48:37,150
so next come the pictures from space.

706
00:48:37,950 --> 00:48:41,125
So what did satellites
see of Amphan's flood?

707
00:48:41,475 --> 00:48:45,556
For days after landfall,
thick clouds covered the ground,

708
00:48:45,556 --> 00:48:48,850
so ordinary pictures
from space can't show it.

709
00:48:49,200 --> 00:48:53,946
And the flooded patches are small, so
the coarse pixels of those pictures,

710
00:48:53,946 --> 00:48:58,500
two hundred and fifty metres wide, blur
them into the land around them.

711
00:48:58,850 --> 00:49:01,300
But radar sees through clouds.

712
00:49:01,650 --> 00:49:05,673
The Sentinel-1 radar satellite
sends out microwaves and

713
00:49:05,673 --> 00:49:08,525
measures how much of them bounces back.

714
00:49:08,875 --> 00:49:11,854
Calm water reflects the microwaves away,

715
00:49:11,854 --> 00:49:15,950
like a mirror, so in a radar
picture, water looks dark.

716
00:49:16,300 --> 00:49:21,147
Here's the coast near Satkhira and the
Sundarbans on the sixteenth of May,

717
00:49:21,147 --> 00:49:26,125
before Amphan, and on the twenty second,
two and a half days after landfall.

718
00:49:27,625 --> 00:49:33,400
As the line moves across, new dark patches
appear where the fields were dry before.

719
00:49:33,750 --> 00:49:37,775
We count the pixels that turned
dark, marked here in cyan:

720
00:49:37,775 --> 00:49:41,175
about thirty four thousand
seven hundred of them.

721
00:49:41,525 --> 00:49:45,090
Each pixel is a square
sixty metres on each side,

722
00:49:45,090 --> 00:49:49,600
so it covers zero point zero
zero three six square kilometres.

723
00:49:49,950 --> 00:49:54,287
The number of dark pixels times
the area of one pixel is about

724
00:49:54,287 --> 00:49:58,275
a hundred and twenty five
square kilometres of new water.

725
00:49:59,775 --> 00:50:04,100
That's about the size of a square
eleven kilometres on each side.

726
00:50:05,600 --> 00:50:09,082
Some of the best-known
flood pictures from space of

727
00:50:09,082 --> 00:50:12,427
a Bay of Bengal cyclone
come from Cyclone Nargis,

728
00:50:12,427 --> 00:50:14,475
in May two thousand and eight.

729
00:50:14,825 --> 00:50:18,775
Let's go to Myanmar,
on the eastern side of the Bay.

730
00:50:19,125 --> 00:50:23,508
Here, the Irrawaddy River, which
Myanmar calls the Ayeyarwady,

731
00:50:23,508 --> 00:50:27,325
fans out into many channels
before it reaches the sea.

732
00:50:27,675 --> 00:50:32,311
This is the Irrawaddy delta:
a huge, flat plain of rice fields,

733
00:50:32,311 --> 00:50:35,475
most of it only a few
metres above the sea.

734
00:50:35,825 --> 00:50:39,675
Nargis formed in the south
of the Bay in late April.

735
00:50:40,025 --> 00:50:45,250
It moved west, then north, then turned
east, straight towards the delta.

736
00:50:45,600 --> 00:50:50,413
On the second of May, it came ashore
at the delta's south-western corner,

737
00:50:50,413 --> 00:50:54,500
with winds of about ninety knots,
averaged over three minutes.

738
00:50:54,850 --> 00:50:58,626
A knot is one point eight five two
kilometres per hour,

739
00:50:58,626 --> 00:51:03,500
so ninety knots is about a hundred
and sixty seven kilometres per hour.

740
00:51:03,850 --> 00:51:09,360
A kilometre per hour is a thousand metres
in three thousand six hundred seconds,

741
00:51:09,360 --> 00:51:14,525
so we divide by three point six, and get
about forty six metres per second.

742
00:51:16,025 --> 00:51:19,014
Then it crossed the whole delta, eastward,

743
00:51:19,014 --> 00:51:22,075
towards Yangon, the country's
largest city.

744
00:51:22,425 --> 00:51:27,847
Here's Nargis that day, seen by a NASA
satellite a few hours before landfall,

745
00:51:27,847 --> 00:51:30,100
with its eye just off the coast.

746
00:51:30,450 --> 00:51:35,400
Its storm surge and heavy rain
flooded the low delta, far inland.

747
00:51:35,750 --> 00:51:38,626
Before we look
at the flood, let's see which

748
00:51:38,626 --> 00:51:41,175
way its wind blew on the delta's coast.

749
00:51:41,975 --> 00:51:46,584
We use ERA5 again, at eleven
in the morning, universal time,

750
00:51:46,584 --> 00:51:50,425
on the second of May, around
the time of landfall.

751
00:51:50,775 --> 00:51:53,252
Again, ERA5's storm is too weak,

752
00:51:53,252 --> 00:51:58,825
at only about half of the official
wind, so we use it for the direction.

753
00:51:59,175 --> 00:52:03,550
Our point is on the delta's
coast, south of the town of Bogale.

754
00:52:03,900 --> 00:52:06,164
Going west along the coast there,

755
00:52:06,164 --> 00:52:09,936
we move about sixty
nine point five kilometres west and

756
00:52:09,936 --> 00:52:12,200
ten point eight kilometres north.

757
00:52:12,550 --> 00:52:16,127
The north leg over the west
leg gives the tangent,

758
00:52:16,127 --> 00:52:19,775
so the coast runs about
nine degrees north of west.

759
00:52:20,125 --> 00:52:22,065
Measured clockwise from north,

760
00:52:22,065 --> 00:52:25,750
that's a bearing of two hundred
and seventy nine degrees.

761
00:52:26,100 --> 00:52:29,056
So alpha, ninety degrees
minus the bearing,

762
00:52:29,056 --> 00:52:33,250
plus a full turn, is about a hundred
and seventy one degrees,

763
00:52:33,250 --> 00:52:37,375
with a cosine of about
minus zero point nine eight eight and

764
00:52:37,375 --> 00:52:40,125
a sine of about zero point one five six.

765
00:52:40,475 --> 00:52:43,875
Then y hat prime points south, out to sea.

766
00:52:44,225 --> 00:52:48,425
ERA5's wind there was about
nineteen metres per second,

767
00:52:48,425 --> 00:52:50,411
from the south-south-west:

768
00:52:50,411 --> 00:52:55,375
u about plus nine point six, and v about
plus sixteen point five.

769
00:52:55,725 --> 00:53:01,925
Across the shore, we take minus u sine
alpha, and v cosine alpha, and add them.

770
00:53:02,275 --> 00:53:05,784
Together, that's about
minus seventeen point eight

771
00:53:05,784 --> 00:53:08,100
metres per second: onto the land.

772
00:53:09,600 --> 00:53:14,963
The official lowest pressure was about
nine hundred and sixty two hectopascals,

773
00:53:14,963 --> 00:53:18,900
so the sea under the eye rose
about fifty one centimetres.

774
00:53:19,250 --> 00:53:21,885
So Nargis drove the sea onto the delta,

775
00:53:21,885 --> 00:53:25,600
where most of the land lies
only a few metres above it.

776
00:53:25,950 --> 00:53:29,785
There's no tide-gauge record
of the water there in our data,

777
00:53:29,785 --> 00:53:32,150
so next come the pictures from space.

778
00:53:32,500 --> 00:53:36,800
Let's zoom in, to see the delta
before and after the storm.

779
00:53:37,600 --> 00:53:43,350
First, here's the delta in natural colour,
the way our eyes would see it from space.

780
00:53:43,700 --> 00:53:49,000
On the left is the twenty first
of April, eleven days before landfall.

781
00:53:49,350 --> 00:53:53,375
On the right is the fifth
of May, about three days after.

782
00:53:54,875 --> 00:53:58,300
As the line glides
across, look for the flood.

783
00:53:58,650 --> 00:54:04,700
In natural colour, it's hard to see: muddy
floodwater and wet soil both look brown.

784
00:54:05,050 --> 00:54:07,825
So flood mappers use false colour.

785
00:54:08,175 --> 00:54:13,293
The satellite measures light that our
eyes can't see, shortwave infrared and

786
00:54:13,293 --> 00:54:15,987
near infrared, and we paint them red and

787
00:54:15,987 --> 00:54:18,950
green, with ordinary
red light painted blue.

788
00:54:19,300 --> 00:54:23,349
In these colours, plants look
bright green, dry fields and

789
00:54:23,349 --> 00:54:28,725
bare ground look pink, clouds look
white, and water looks dark blue or black.

790
00:54:29,075 --> 00:54:32,282
Water soaks up both
kinds of infrared light,

791
00:54:32,282 --> 00:54:35,125
so it stays dark, even when it's muddy.

792
00:54:35,475 --> 00:54:37,925
That makes water easy to spot.

793
00:54:38,275 --> 00:54:41,275
Here are the same two
days in false colour.

794
00:54:41,625 --> 00:54:43,530
As the line glides across,

795
00:54:43,530 --> 00:54:47,925
dark water spreads over land
that was pink and green before.

796
00:54:49,425 --> 00:54:52,792
Both pictures are pinned
to the same latitudes and

797
00:54:52,792 --> 00:54:56,900
longitudes, so every pixel
compares the same patch of ground.

798
00:54:57,250 --> 00:55:03,050
Even so, the picture shows what the surge
left behind, not the surge itself.

799
00:55:03,850 --> 00:55:09,100
Our last flood is on the other side
of the Indian Ocean, south of the equator.

800
00:55:09,450 --> 00:55:14,930
In March twenty nineteen, Cyclone Idai
grew strong in the Mozambique Channel,

801
00:55:14,930 --> 00:55:17,350
between Madagascar and Mozambique.

802
00:55:17,700 --> 00:55:22,990
South of the equator, a tropical cyclone
turns clockwise as seen from above,

803
00:55:22,990 --> 00:55:26,400
the opposite way to the storms
we've seen so far.

804
00:55:26,750 --> 00:55:29,476
Here's Idai on the fourteenth of March,

805
00:55:29,476 --> 00:55:32,900
the day it came
ashore, seen by a NASA satellite.

806
00:55:33,250 --> 00:55:37,018
It reached the coast near
the city of Beira around midnight,

807
00:55:37,018 --> 00:55:38,651
between the fourteenth and

808
00:55:38,651 --> 00:55:43,675
the fifteenth, with winds of about eighty
five knots, averaged over ten minutes.

809
00:55:44,025 --> 00:55:46,542
With the knot converted as for Nargis,

810
00:55:46,542 --> 00:55:50,450
that's about a hundred and fifty
seven kilometres per hour.

811
00:55:50,800 --> 00:55:54,400
And in metres
per second, it's about forty four.

812
00:55:55,900 --> 00:55:58,853
Beira and the town of Buzi sit on low,

813
00:55:58,853 --> 00:56:02,350
flat land where two big
rivers reach the sea.

814
00:56:02,700 --> 00:56:04,450
Let's zoom in.

815
00:56:05,250 --> 00:56:09,893
Here's ERA5's wind at seven
in the evening, universal time,

816
00:56:09,893 --> 00:56:14,300
on the fourteenth of March, a few
hours before landfall.

817
00:56:14,650 --> 00:56:18,886
Its strongest wind is about two
thirds of the official estimate,

818
00:56:18,886 --> 00:56:21,600
so once more we use it for the direction.

819
00:56:21,950 --> 00:56:26,883
Idai turns clockwise, as seen
from above, so on its western side,

820
00:56:26,883 --> 00:56:30,450
where Beira was, the wind
blew in from the sea.

821
00:56:30,800 --> 00:56:33,275
Our point is Beira's seafront.

822
00:56:33,625 --> 00:56:36,215
Going south-west along the coast there,

823
00:56:36,215 --> 00:56:39,868
we move about fifty
two point three kilometres west and

824
00:56:39,868 --> 00:56:42,525
thirty five point nine kilometres south.

825
00:56:42,875 --> 00:56:46,301
The south leg over the west
leg gives the tangent,

826
00:56:46,301 --> 00:56:50,275
so the coast runs about thirty
four degrees south of west.

827
00:56:50,625 --> 00:56:52,601
Measured clockwise from north,

828
00:56:52,601 --> 00:56:56,225
that's a bearing of two
hundred and thirty six degrees.

829
00:56:56,575 --> 00:56:59,584
So alpha, ninety degrees
minus the bearing,

830
00:56:59,584 --> 00:57:03,783
plus a full turn, is about two
hundred and fourteen degrees,

831
00:57:03,783 --> 00:57:07,841
with a cosine of about
minus zero point eight two nine and

832
00:57:07,841 --> 00:57:11,200
a sine of about
minus zero point five five nine.

833
00:57:11,550 --> 00:57:15,250
Then y hat prime points
south-east, out to sea.

834
00:57:15,600 --> 00:57:19,798
ERA5's wind there was about
twenty two metres per second,

835
00:57:19,798 --> 00:57:21,714
from the south-south-east:

836
00:57:21,714 --> 00:57:26,575
u about minus six point seven, and v about
plus twenty point nine.

837
00:57:26,925 --> 00:57:33,125
Across the shore, we take minus u sine
alpha, and v cosine alpha, and add them.

838
00:57:33,475 --> 00:57:38,725
Together, that's about minus twenty
one metres per second: onto the land.

839
00:57:40,225 --> 00:57:45,596
The official lowest pressure was about
nine hundred and fifty five hectopascals,

840
00:57:45,596 --> 00:57:49,625
so the sea under the eye rose
about fifty eight centimetres.

841
00:57:49,975 --> 00:57:55,950
So Idai's wind pushed the sea onto Beira's
coast, and into the mouths of its rivers.

842
00:57:56,300 --> 00:58:00,135
There's no tide-gauge record
of the water there in our data,

843
00:58:00,135 --> 00:58:02,500
so next come the pictures from space.

844
00:58:03,300 --> 00:58:06,396
Here's the land round
Beira in natural colour,

845
00:58:06,396 --> 00:58:08,752
on the eleventh of March, three and

846
00:58:08,752 --> 00:58:13,800
a half days before landfall, and on
the twenty fifth, about ten days after.

847
00:58:15,300 --> 00:58:19,150
Again, the flood is hard
to see in natural colour.

848
00:58:19,500 --> 00:58:22,400
Now the same two days in false colour.

849
00:58:22,750 --> 00:58:27,201
Dark blue water covers wide plains
along the rivers round Beira,

850
00:58:27,201 --> 00:58:29,775
where there were green fields before.

851
00:58:31,275 --> 00:58:34,200
On the left, the white blobs are clouds.

852
00:58:34,550 --> 00:58:39,350
How to map floods from space has
its own movie later in this series.

853
00:58:40,150 --> 00:58:44,839
To sum up, the tip of r hat moves
along a circle of radius one,

854
00:58:44,839 --> 00:58:50,050
so for a small turn delta theta, it
moves delta theta along theta hat.

855
00:58:50,400 --> 00:58:55,675
That gave us the slopes of cosine
and sine: minus sine, and cosine.

856
00:58:56,025 --> 00:59:02,305
So d r hat by d theta is theta hat, and d
theta hat by d theta is minus r hat:

857
00:59:02,305 --> 00:59:05,525
each arrow swings towards the other one.

858
00:59:05,875 --> 00:59:11,050
The product rule says that a number times
an arrow changes through both of them.

859
00:59:11,400 --> 00:59:16,992
On a ring, the angle grows
at v over r, the velocity is v theta hat,

860
00:59:16,992 --> 00:59:20,857
and the acceleration
is minus v squared over r,

861
00:59:20,857 --> 00:59:24,475
times r hat: inward, even
at a steady speed.

862
00:59:24,825 --> 00:59:30,442
For Ian's ring, that's about zero point
zero four eight metres per second squared,

863
00:59:30,442 --> 00:59:33,525
about two hundred times
smaller than gravity.

864
00:59:33,875 --> 00:59:37,415
Adding the height z gives
cylindrical coordinates,

865
00:59:37,415 --> 00:59:41,664
and averaging round the rings
shows a tropical cyclone's in,

866
00:59:41,664 --> 00:59:44,425
up and out, in Ian and in Amphan alike.

867
00:59:44,775 --> 00:59:50,074
Turning the axes to fit the coast split
Ian's wind into its parts along and

868
00:59:50,074 --> 00:59:51,275
across the shore.

869
00:59:51,625 --> 00:59:53,011
At Fort Myers Beach,

870
00:59:53,011 --> 00:59:57,516
about thirty one point five metres
per second blew onto the land,

871
00:59:57,516 --> 01:00:02,575
and the gauge up the river at Fort
Myers rose about two point two metres.

872
01:00:02,925 --> 01:00:06,925
At Saint Petersburg, about
twelve point one blew out to sea,

873
01:00:06,925 --> 01:00:10,125
and the water fell about
one point eight metres.

874
01:00:10,475 --> 01:00:13,977
Before the floods after
Amphan, Nargis and Idai,

875
01:00:13,977 --> 01:00:17,260
the across-shore wind
blew onto the land too:

876
01:00:17,260 --> 01:00:20,909
about twenty two point four,
seventeen point eight

877
01:00:20,909 --> 01:00:23,900
and twenty one metres per second in ERA5.

878
01:00:24,250 --> 01:00:27,212
Radar saw Amphan's flood
through the clouds,

879
01:00:27,212 --> 01:00:31,050
and false colour showed
the floods after Nargis and Idai.

880
01:00:31,400 --> 01:00:37,025
In Part 3, we'll take these ideas to the
biggest round shape of all: the Earth.

881
01:00:37,375 --> 01:00:42,043
There, we'll learn to turn latitudes
and longitudes into kilometres,

882
01:00:42,043 --> 01:00:46,300
the rule behind the kilometre
maps we used for today's coasts.

883
01:00:47,100 --> 01:00:51,258
This video is one of a series
of related maths tutorials,

884
01:00:51,258 --> 01:00:53,300
so watch out for the others.

885
01:00:53,650 --> 01:00:58,047
They're made for students
of atmospheric science, meteorology and

886
01:00:58,047 --> 01:01:01,700
fluid dynamics, and anyone
else who needs these ideas.

887
01:01:02,050 --> 01:01:06,878
The notebook for coordinates, and the
notebooks for the other maths concepts,

888
01:01:06,878 --> 01:01:09,700
are on the website that
goes with this video.
